<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Tuʻi Tonga Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">950 CE – 1865 CE (approx.) · capital Muʻa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The line of sacred Tongan paramount kings who, from Muʻa on Tongatapu, built a seaborne network of power and prestige reaching Uvea, Samoa and Fiji.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tongan tradition traces the line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahoeitu">ʻAhoʻeitu</a>, son of the sky god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagaloa">Tangaloa</a> and a mortal woman, who became the first Tuʻi <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tonga">Tonga</a> around 950. Early rulers lived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongatapu">Tongatapu</a>, and by the 12th or 13th century the royal centre had moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mua-tonga">Muʻa</a> on the lagoon shore, where the great stone-faced tombs (langi) of later kings still stand.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Tuʻi <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tonga">Tonga</a> was a sacred figure, owed the first fruits of the yearly ʻinasi offering. Around 1200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuitatui">Tuʻitātui</a> is credited with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haamonga-a-maui">Haʻamonga ʻa Maui</a> trilithon. Double-hulled canoes carried chiefs, marriages and tribute across a wide arc, with Tongan influence felt in Uvea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futuna-wallis-and-futuna">Futuna</a>, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lau-islands">Lau</a> islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. In the 15th century, after royal assassinations, everyday power passed to a junior line, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-haatakalaua">Tuʻi Haʻatakalaua</a>, and later to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Civil wars in the late 18th and early 19th centuries ended with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-tupou-i">Tāufaʻāhau</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tui-kanokupolu">Tuʻi Kanokupolu</a>, uniting the islands as King George Tupou I and founding the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga</a> in 1845. The last Tuʻi Tonga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laufilitonga">Laufilitonga</a>, died in 1865 and the title was not renewed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tu-i-tonga-empire.jpg" alt="Tuʻi Tonga Empire" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Thommy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tonga_(1862-1866).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>950 CE – 1865 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Muʻa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>ʻAhoʻeitu, Tuʻitātui, Laufilitonga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tongan traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tongan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Tonga</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tui Tonga Empire, Tongan maritime empire, Tuʻi Tonga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=tu-i-tonga-empire" aria-label="Tuʻi Tonga Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="-27 2961 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tuʻi Tonga Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M293 3217L290 3215M293 3217L169 2928"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M290 3215h0M169 2928h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M293 3217h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M293 3217h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Muʻa. <a href="/atlas?year=1408&amp;focus=tu-i-tonga-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
